      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "text": "**Yes. A component-issue event for manufacturing order `MO-25-09-1070` was created 75 minutes after September entered Pending Close. Its recorded `POSTED` status is inconsistent with Oracle’s documented treatment for this type of newly created transaction.**\n\n### Event identified\n\n| Item | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Manufacturing order | `MO-25-09-1070` |\n| Work order ID | `WO-2025-09-004` |\n| Operation / product | Modine Racine–Dekoven / Cooler Plate Pack |\n| Cost period | `Sep-25` — `PER-COST-2025-09` |\n| Source transaction | `TXN-ISSUE-250930-1070` |\n| Cost event | `CE-250904-0004` — `INPUT` (component issue) |\n| Amount | **USD $284,763.42** — 28,476,342 cents |\n| Exact event-created time | `2025-09-30T19:15:00` |\n| Exact Pending Close time | `2025-09-30T18:00:00` |\n| Time after Pending Close | **75 minutes** — 1 hour 15 minutes |\n| Recorded accounting status | **`POSTED`** |\n\nThe elapsed-time calculation is **4,500 seconds ÷ 60 = 75 minutes**. Both timestamps are reproduced exactly as recorded; the source provides no time-zone designation.\n\n### Policy conclusion\n\nOracle’s Pending Close guidance states that newly created transactions falling in the current or an earlier period **will not be staged or processed until the period is closed or reopened**. Revenue lines, COGS recognition, and manual cost adjustments are listed exceptions.\n\nThis event is a new **component-issue `INPUT` transaction**, not one of those exceptions. Based on the supplied records, its **`POSTED` status is therefore not consistent** with the documented Pending Close treatment.\n\n**Sources:** Supplied `work_orders`, `cost_periods`, and `work_order_cost_events` records from `system/oracle_scm_cost_management/state.db`; Oracle Supply Chain Cost Management 26B, “Cost Accounting Period Statuses and Transaction Accounting,” PDF page 233 (printed page 225).\n\nThe review was read-only; **no database records were changed**.",
